1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3  (3. f4 {3 pawns attack}  c5 {often against the 3 pawns attack, you strike with c5 quickly Notice how black has the dragon setup. this gives the f5 push less of a bite and makes it look overextended rather than scary.} 4. dxc5  (4. c3?!  cxd4 5. cxd4 d5 6. e5 {[%cal Gg8h6,Gc8f5,Gb8c6,Gf7f6]now white has overextended and the light squares are up for grabs. Black plans to put his knight on h6 and then at some point break with f6 and white is overextended and his kind is airy.})  (4. d5  d6 {now we enter a benoni-like structure.} 5. Nf3 Nf6 6. Nc3  (6. e5?!  dxe5 7. fxe5 Nxd5 {if white overextends black wins a pawn}) O-O 7. Bd3 b5!  (7... e6  ) 8. Nxb5  (8. Bxb5  ) c4 9. Bxc4 Nxe4 10. O-O Qb6+ 11. Nbd4) Qa5+ 5. Bd2 Qxc5 {[%cal Ge7e7,Gd7d7,Ge4e4,Gc5g1]you should be happy to see this on the board. You now have two central pawns (d7 and e7) against White’s single e4 pawn. White has no d-pawn to contest the center. Your long-term plan is simple: develop normally with ...Nc6 and ...d6, and eventually dictate the center. Because the e4 pawn lacks pawn support, it often becomes a chronic weakness rather than a dangerous attacking wedge. Furthermore with f4 they have weakened the g1 a7 diagonal and castuling will be awkward with my queen eyeing down those squares. This doesn't resemble a grand prix quite because there is no e pawn for white, and the grand prix typically arises in closed sicilians with e3.} 6. Bc3  (6. Nc3  d6 7. Nf3 Nf6 8. Bb5+  (8. Bd3 {[%cal Ge8g8,Gc8g4,Gb8c6,Gg4f3,Gf6h5]}  O-O {[%cal Gb8c6]}) Nc6) Nf6) d6 4. f4 {Nf6 is the engine best move, but practically according to the database white has a very pleasant game, black should continue playing in a typical modern style with a6 b4.} a6 5. Nf3  (5. a4  Nc6! {if white tries to clamp down on the b4 expansion, then nc6 exploits the now weakened b4 square.} 6. Nf3 Bg4 {adding pressure to the d4 pawn} 7. Be3  (7. d5  Nd4 8. Be2 Nxe2 9. Qxe2 e6 10. dxe6 fxe6) e5 {striking at the centre to further pressure d4}) b5 6. Bd3  (6. a4 {If white goes for an early a4, there are ideas for black to sac the b pawn for activity.}  b4 7. Nd5 Bb7 8. Bd3 Nd7 9. Nxb4 c5 10. dxc5 Nxc5 {[%cal Gd8b6,Gc5e4,Gb7e4,Gg7b2]the pressure on white's centre, b pawn and qb6 incoming makes this slightly better for black despite being down a pawn} 11. O-O  (11. e5  Nh6! {essentially in the 3 pawns attack, whenever they push e5, nh6 is the thematic idea.} 12. O-O Qb6) Nxd3 12. Nxd3  (12. cxd3  )) Bb7 7. a4  (7. O-O  Nd7 8. e5 Nh6 9. e6  (9. Ne4  c5 10. e6 fxe6 11. Neg5 Bd5) fxe6 10. Ng5) c5! {the idea is to } 8. axb5  (8. dxc5  b4 9. Nd5 Bxd5  (9... dxc5  10. O-O Nf6) 10. exd5 Nf6 11. cxd6 Qxd6) cxd4 9. Ne2 Nf6 10. e5 dxe5 11. fxe5 Ng4 *

